# Line endings that actually matter.
#
# Shell scripts are executed by dash/bash on Linux and macOS. With CRLF endings
# the shebang line and `set -e` break, and the script dies with
#   build.sh: 3: set: Illegal option -
# on every Linux machine. That happens whenever the repository is checked out on
# Windows (core.autocrlf=true) and the tree is then copied, mounted or zipped
# over to Linux, so pin LF for the shell scripts explicitly.
#
# Windows batch files are the mirror case: cmd.exe chokes on LF-only line
# endings in some constructs (labels, multi-line blocks), so keep CRLF there.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.command  text eol=lf
Makefile   text eol=lf
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.cmd      text eol=crlf
